SUMMARY:Civic Meetup #562: Ban the Kid\, Keep their Data
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T210000
UID:3b93d6fd05e20339490fa7b712848bdc@guild.host
DESCRIPTION:New technologies and organizations’ practices are regularly 
 placing pressures and challenges on our privacy and transparency rights. T
 his month\, Civic Tech Toronto and the Information and Privacy Commissione
 r of Ontario are highlighting the work to defend\, enforce\, explain and u
 pdate these rights. To see all September events\, check our events listing
  on Guild.&#x20\;\n\n‎‎\n\nTopic: Ban the Kid\, Keep their Data\n\nGov
 ernments in Canada and abroad are moving to protect children online: banni
 ng kids from social media\, mandating safer defaults\, restricting what pl
 atforms can recommend to young users. Much of it depends on first establis
 hing how old users are. In this talk\, Sara Grimes looks at the infrastruc
 ture these proposals routinely require\, including AI-driven facial estima
 tion and behavioural inference\, applied to everyone\, collecting more dat
 a from children in the name of protecting them. She'll ask what the eviden
 ce says about whether these technologies work and what their spreading imp
 lementation means for children’s privacy and other rights.\n\n‎‎\n\n
 Speaker: Sara M. Grimes\, PhD \\[joining remotely]\n\nSara Grimes is a ful
 l professor in Communication Studies at McGill University\, where she hold
 s the Wolfe Chair in Scientific and Technological Literacy and directs the
  Kids Play Tech Lab. For over two decades she has tracked how digital game
 s and social media platforms collect data from young users\, and how polic
 y and design decisions quietly shape what rights children get to exercise.
  She's the author of Digital Playgrounds (University of Toronto Press\, 20
 21) and co-author of the CIFAR policy brief Responsible AI and Children. S
 he regularly works with policymakers in Canada and internationally on chil
 dren’s privacy. // LinkedIn / kidsplaytech.com\n\n‎\n\nAgenda:\n\n7:00
 -7:20 Welcome and Introductions\n\n7:20-7:50 Presentation and Q\\&A/discus
 sion\n\n7:50-9:00 Breakout groups\n\n‎\n\nCode of Conduct: https://civic
 tech.ca/about-us\n\n‎\n\nCheck in with us on the Civic Tech Toronto Slac
 k: https://link.civictech.ca/chat\n\n‎\n\nAbout Us:\n\nOur weekly civic 
 meetups bring together Torontonians (designers\, coders\, urban planners\,
  government staff\, mappers\, policy-makers\, students\, communications st
 rategists\, community organizers\, and more) who share an interest in maki
 ng Toronto more responsive\, prosperous\, sustainable\, and equitable thro
 ugh design\, tech\, and data.\n\nCome and be part of it!\n\nFor more info:
  https://civictech.ca\n\n\\--\n\nInterested in speaking at a future event\
 , or know someone who'd be a great speaker? We'd love to hear from you:\n\
 nhttps://link.civictech.ca/speaker-suggestions\n\nHave a space that could 
 host up to 80 civic-minded folks for an evening? Consider recommending you
 r venue:\n\nhttps://link.civictech.ca/venue-suggestions\n\nVenue Entrances
 :\n\nFrom Bloor St or subway concourse\, or from Yonge St (just beside Jac
 k Astor's)\n\nBloor-01\n\nBloor-02\n\nYonge\n
LOCATION:Online: https://link.civictech.ca/zoom / Information and Privacy 
 Commissioner of Ontario
